    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>A screened porch is the middle ground between the comfort of your living room and the freshness of the garden. It gives you somewhere to enjoy the breeze and the sound of rain without mosquitoes, flies or the worst of the weather. It is also a significant structural project: unlike a low floating deck, a screened porch usually has a roof, is often attached to the house and carries real loads. This guide walks through how a screened porch is built, stage by stage, so you can plan properly, understand what each trade is doing and decide which parts you can realistically take on yourself.</p>

<h2 id="safety-and-professionals">Before you start: approvals and professionals</h2>
<p>Treat this as an overview, not a substitute for a design. A roofed, attached structure almost always needs some form of approval. In England, a porch or veranda may need planning permission or may fall under permitted development, depending on its size, height and position; attached roofed structures typically also need Building Regulations approval. In the US, most jurisdictions require a building permit. Rules vary, and listed buildings, conservation areas and flats have extra restrictions, so check with your local planning or building authority before you design anything in detail.</p>
<p>We strongly recommend a structural engineer or qualified builder for the footings, the ledger attachment to the house, the roof structure and the load calculations (including wind and snow loads). Any wiring should be done by a qualified electrician: in the UK, outdoor circuits must meet Part P of the Building Regulations and BS 7671, including RCD (GFCI in the US) protection. Many confident DIYers still do the screening, finishing and decorating themselves, which is where most of the visible character comes from.</p>

<h2 id="planning">1. Planning</h2>
<p>The success of any structural project lies in the preparation. Before you strike a single nail, talk to your local planning and building control department. You will usually need a site plan and construction drawings that meet local regulations on structure, wind and snow loads, drainage and distances to boundaries. An engineer or designer can prepare these and specify footing sizes, timber sizes and fixings for your site.</p>
<p>Think about orientation too. A south-facing porch gets the most sun but can become uncomfortably hot in summer without good ventilation or a ceiling fan; a north-facing porch will be cooler and shadier. At this stage, also decide on the screening system: traditional staples covered with timber trim, or a track-and-spline system. Track systems tend to give tighter screens and make it easier to replace a damaged panel later.</p>
<!--figure:floor-plan-sketching|Hand sketching on a floor plan beside a 3D model on a laptop: working out the layout and orientation on paper before drawings go to building control-->

<h2 id="materials">2. Materials</h2>
<p>A porch needs durable materials designed to cope with the weather. For framing, pressure-treated timber is the usual choice because it resists rot and insect attack. For a more decorative finish, many homeowners choose cedar or redwood for the visible posts and rafters. Final sizes should come from your engineer or span tables for your timber grade, but typical materials include:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Floor joists:</strong> pressure-treated 47 × 200 mm (2×8) or 47 × 250 mm (2×10).</li>
<li><strong>Support posts:</strong> 100 × 100 mm (4×4) or 150 × 150 mm (6×6).</li>
<li><strong>Screening mesh:</strong> fibreglass is common and easy to work with; aluminium is more durable but harder to tension.</li>
<li><strong>Fixings:</strong> stainless steel or hot-dip galvanised screws, nails and connectors, compatible with treated timber, to prevent corrosion.</li>
<li><strong>Roofing:</strong> match the existing house roof covering where you can, or choose metal sheeting for a more rustic look, subject to approval.</li>
<li><strong>Screening system:</strong> a track-and-spline system or staples and timber trim.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="foundation">3. Foundation</h2>
<p>The foundation is the most critical part of the structure. Most screened porches sit on concrete footings that go deep enough to avoid movement from frost and soil shrinkage. The depth depends on your soil, local frost depth and local regulations, and should be set by your engineer or agreed with building control; do not guess it. Before any digging, check for buried cables, pipes and drains.</p>
<p>The layout is marked out with batter boards and string lines, and checked for square with the 3-4-5 triangle method (or by measuring both diagonals). Concrete is poured into the holes, often using cardboard tube formwork, and galvanised post anchors are set into the wet concrete. Once the concrete has cured, the posts are fixed to the anchors and checked with a level so they are perfectly plumb: any lean at the base is magnified by the time you reach the roofline.</p>

<h2 id="framing">4. Framing</h2>
<p>With the posts in place, the floor and wall framing follow. If the porch is attached to the house, a ledger board is fixed to the house structure. This is one of the most important connections in the build and should be designed by an engineer: how it is fixed depends on what the house wall is made of. Proper flashing above the ledger is essential to stop water getting behind it and damaging the house.</p>
<p>Rim joists go on next, then the inner joists, typically at 400 mm (16 in) centres; heavier floors, such as stone or thick boards, may need 300 mm (12 in) centres. Build in a fall of about 1 in 100 (roughly 1/8 in per foot) away from the house so rainwater runs off. Once the frame is complete, the floor goes down: tongue-and-groove porch flooring, or standard decking with fine insect mesh fixed underneath so insects cannot crawl up through the gaps.</p>

<h2 id="roofing">5. Roofing</h2>
<p>The roof is usually the most challenging part of the build, and the part most worth handing to a professional. The common choices are a lean-to (single slope, often called a shed roof) or a pitched gable roof with two slopes meeting at a ridge. A gable roof feels more open and can improve airflow.</p>
<p>On a gable roof, the ridge beam is installed first, supported as your engineer specifies. Rafters are cut with a birdsmouth notch so they sit securely on the wall plates, then fixed with the specified connectors. Exterior-grade plywood or OSB sheathing follows, then an underlay or membrane, then the roof covering. Drip edges and gutters carry rainwater away from the footings and the house. Working at height brings its own risks, so use proper scaffolding or access equipment rather than ladders alone.</p>

<h2 id="screening">6. Screening</h2>
<p>Once the structure is weathertight, the screening can begin. This is where the project starts to look like a porch, and it is a good stage for a careful DIYer. With a track system, screw the base tracks to the vertical and horizontal framing.</p>
<p>Cut each piece of mesh slightly larger than the opening. Start at the top, pressing the mesh into the track with a spline roller and rubber spline, then pull it taut as you work down the sides and along the bottom. You are aiming for a tight, wrinkle-free finish. With the traditional method, staple the mesh to the timber and cover the staples with thin timber trim strips. Remember to leave one opening for the screen door, framed to the exact size of the door you have chosen.</p>

<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Tension the mesh in opposite directions as you go, top then bottom, left then right, rather than working all the way round one side. It keeps the weave straight and avoids ripples at the corners.</p></blockquote>

<h2 id="finishing">7. Finishing</h2>
<p>The final phase covers the details that make the space usable. Sand any rough edges on the posts and apply a good-quality exterior stain or paint, following the product's instructions; it protects the timber from moisture and UV as well as looking smart.</p>
<p>Hang the screen door so it swings freely and closes securely with a latch or magnetic catch. This is also when your electrician wires in overhead lighting, a ceiling fan and outdoor sockets with RCD (GFCI in the US) protection. A ceiling fan is well worth including: it creates a breeze on still days and makes it harder for small insects to settle. Finally, bring in furniture, an outdoor rug, cushions and soft lighting to turn the build into a room you will want to use.</p>

<h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
<h3>Do I really need permission for a screened porch?</h3>
<p>Almost certainly you need to check. In England a porch or veranda may need planning permission or may be permitted development depending on its size and position, and an attached roofed structure typically needs Building Regulations approval. In the US, most jurisdictions require a permit. Building without the right approvals can cause problems when you sell and, in some cases, enforcement action. Ask your local planning or building authority first.</p>
<h3>What is the best screen material?</h3>
<p>Fibreglass is popular because it is flexible, easy to install and does not crease. If you have pets, a heavier pet-resistant mesh (usually vinyl-coated polyester) stands up better to claws. Near the coast, bronze or stainless steel mesh resists salt-air corrosion better.</p>
<h3>How do I keep the porch floor dry?</h3>
<p>Screens keep out insects but not all rain. Give the floor a slight fall of about 1 in 100 (roughly 1/8 in per foot) away from the house, use gutters on the roof and seal or finish the flooring as its manufacturer recommends so water runs off rather than soaking in.</p>
<h3>Can I convert my existing deck into a screened porch?</h3>
<p>Sometimes, but it must be assessed by a structural engineer first. A roof adds significant weight, plus wind and snow loads, that most decks were never designed to carry. The footings, posts, beams and ledger may all need upgrading, and the conversion will usually need approval in its own right.</p>
<h3>How long does it take to build a screened porch?</h3>
<p>It varies widely with the size, the roof design, the site, the weather and how much you hand to professionals. For a DIY-led build it often runs to several weekends, plus time for drawings, approvals and inspections at key stages.</p>

<h2 id="final-thoughts">Final thoughts</h2>
<p>A screened porch can give you a genuinely new room for most of the year, but it rewards careful planning and the right help with the structural work. Start by sketching your layout and speaking to your local planning or building office, then bring in an engineer before you order materials. Once it is built, our guide to <a href="/blog/screened-porch-maintenance-by-season">screened porch maintenance by season</a> will help you keep it in good shape, and if you would rather start with something simpler, read <a href="/blog/how-to-build-a-floating-deck">how to build a floating deck</a> or check the common <a href="/blog/deck-railing-mistakes">deck railing mistakes</a> to avoid. For help furnishing and styling the finished space, explore our <a href="/services/balcony-terrace-decoration">balcony and terrace decoration</a> service or <a href="/book-consultation">book a consultation</a>.</p>
